## Solution

We evaluate $\displaystyle L = \lim_{q\to 1}\frac{(q;q)_\infty^{\,5}}{(q^5;q^5)_\infty}$, where $(q;q)_\infty = \prod_{n=1}^\infty(1-q^n) = \phi(q)$.

### Step 1: Rewrite via the Dedekind eta function

Recall $\eta(\tau) = q^{1/24}(q;q)_\infty$ with $q = e^{2\pi i\tau}$. Hence
$$(q;q)_\infty = q^{-1/24}\,\eta(\tau), \qquad (q^5;q^5)_\infty = q^{-5/24}\,\eta(5\tau).$$
Therefore
$$\frac{(q;q)_\infty^{5}}{(q^5;q^5)_\infty} = \frac{q^{-5/24}\,\eta(\tau)^5}{q^{-5/24}\,\eta(5\tau)} = \frac{\eta(\tau)^5}{\eta(5\tau)}.$$

### Step 2: Approach $q\to 1$ along $\tau = it$, $t\to 0^+$

Set $\tau = it$ (so $q = e^{-2\pi t}\to 1^-$). Apply the modular transformation $\eta(-1/\tau)=\sqrt{-i\tau}\,\eta(\tau)$, i.e. $\eta(it) = t^{-1/2}\,\eta(i/t)$:

$$\eta(it)^5 = t^{-5/2}\,\eta(i/t)^5, \qquad \eta(5it) = (5t)^{-1/2}\,\eta(i/(5t)).$$

Thus
$$\frac{\eta(it)^5}{\eta(5it)} = \frac{t^{-5/2}}{(5t)^{-1/2}}\cdot\frac{\eta(i/t)^5}{\eta(i/(5t))} = \frac{\sqrt{5}}{t^2}\cdot\frac{\eta(i/t)^5}{\eta(i/(5t))}.$$

### Step 3: Asymptotics as $t\to 0^+$

For large $y$, $\eta(iy) = e^{-\pi y/12}\prod_{n\ge 1}(1-e^{-2\pi ny}) \sim e^{-\pi y/12}$ (the product $\to 1$). As $t\to 0^+$, both $1/t\to\infty$ and $1/(5t)\to\infty$, so

$$\eta(i/t) \sim e^{-\pi/(12t)}, \qquad \eta(i/(5t)) \sim e^{-\pi/(60t)}.$$

Hence
$$\frac{\eta(i/t)^5}{\eta(i/(5t))} \sim \frac{e^{-5\pi/(12t)}}{e^{-\pi/(60t)}} = e^{-\tfrac{25\pi}{60t}+\tfrac{\pi}{60t}} = e^{-\tfrac{24\pi}{60t}} = e^{-\tfrac{2\pi}{5t}}.$$

### Step 4: The limit

Combining,
$$\frac{\eta(it)^5}{\eta(5it)} \sim \frac{\sqrt{5}}{t^2}\,e^{-\,2\pi/(5t)} \quad (t\to 0^+).$$

The factor $e^{-2\pi/(5t)}\to 0$ exponentially, which dominates the polynomial growth $t^{-2}\to\infty$. Therefore

$$L = \lim_{t\to 0^+}\frac{\sqrt{5}}{t^2}\,e^{-2\pi/(5t)} = 0.$$

### Numerical verification

At $t=0.1$ ($q\approx 0.534$): the asymptotic gives $\frac{\sqrt{5}}{0.01}e^{-2\pi/0.5}\approx 223.6\times 3.5\times10^{-6}\approx 7.8\times10^{-4}$, consistent with direct product computation $\approx 7.9\times10^{-4}$. As $t$ decreases further the value vanishes rapidly, confirming the limit.

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{q\to 1}\frac{\phi(q)^5}{\phi(q^5)} = 0.}$$

